fix(browser): drop manager lock before awaiting CDP screenshot

`take_screenshot_for_session` and the legacy `take_screenshot` both held
the manager mutex across `page.screenshot().await`. Any other task that
needed the same lock — the CDP handler task processing events, a
concurrent `get_or_create_session_page`, a `close_page` — would block
for the duration of the screenshot round-trip. Worst case: the handler
task wants the lock during the screenshot → deadlock, browser appears
hung, agent can't continue.

Page is an Arc-wrapped handle that's cheap to clone. New pattern:

    let page = {
        let inner = self.inner.lock().await;
        inner.pages.get(&key)?.clone()
    };
    // lock dropped here
    let bytes = page.screenshot(...).await.ok()?;

Standard "don't hold async locks across await" — applied at both call
sites.

feat(rsi): BinEval-style failure triage in the RSI prompt (#236)

Replace the crude "high failure rate -> write a rule" instinct with a binary
triage the RSI must run before acting, drawn from the BinEval paper's finding
that iterative prompt updates help only when the model has the capability and
needs guidance — and degrade into competing instructions otherwise:

- minimum-sample guard (skip tools with <5 calls — no acting on n=1);
- recoverable/environmental vs real-defect distinction (don't write rules
  about stale-hash retries, not-connected, cancelled prompts);
- misuse vs broken (fix usage, don't avoid the tool);
- capability vs guidance (a rule can't fix a hard limitation);
- never ban a built-in tool — routing guidance only; only tools.toml tools
  may be disabled;
- explicit anti-bloat rule (no violation counters / restated / competing
  rules; refine in place).

style: apply cargo fmt to the telegram send and plan-file paths

Line-wrapping only, no behaviour change. Left over from the telegram PR stack;
committed separately so the three fixes in front of it stay reviewable.
